## 2. Checks on the people already in your draft

**Yakun Xi and Meng Wang are appropriate local anchors.** Both have Zhejiang University profiles identifying harmonic analysis as a research area. Xi’s work on Kakeya–Nikodym problems and eigenfunction estimates provides a particularly concrete scientific connection to mention. I could not verify the proposed “roughly three or four PhD students each,” so I would omit those numbers. ([Zhejiang University Personal Page][6])

**Zhongwei Shen’s Westlake affiliation is correct.** Westlake announced that he joined on **July 1, 2025**, and describes his research as lying at the interface of harmonic analysis, PDEs, and mathematical physics. He is a strong local connection, rather than simply a senior researcher to list. ([Westlake University][7])

For Shanghai, use **Xi Chen**, the English name on his official profile, and write **“the Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences at Fudan University”** rather than treating SCMS and Fudan as separate universities. Chen’s work connects to microlocal analysis, spectral theory, and PDEs; **Danqing He** is a direct harmonic-analysis match through multilinear singular integrals, multipliers, and local smoothing. ([Shanghai Maths Centre][8])

**Bobo Hua is worth retaining when the workshop includes geometric or spectral aspects.** His work on graph Laplacians, heat equations, Riesz transforms, and discrete Schrödinger equations supplies a genuine connection, but I would describe it as **geometric and discrete analysis** rather than group it indistinguishably with classical harmonic analysis. ([Fudan University Maths School][9])

### A concrete regional precedent

The official programme for the **September 5–8, 2025 harmonic-analysis workshop at SCMS** lists **Xi Chen and Danqing He among the organizers**, and **Yakun Xi and Zhongwei Shen among the speakers**. This is stronger evidence than proximity alone: your proposed workshop can be presented as **building on existing Hangzhou–Shanghai scientific exchanges**. 

## 3. Local postdoctoral and PhD-level contacts

**Xuerui Yang (杨学睿)** is a suitable name for the early-career TODO. His current academic homepage identifies him as a **postdoctoral researcher at Zhejiang University**, working in **harmonic analysis and analytic number theory**, including additive-combinatorial questions approached using Fourier-analytic tools. His listed institutional email is **[xueruiy3@zju.edu.cn](mailto:xueruiy3@zju.edu.cn)**. I would describe him as a ZJU postdoc, not an IASM postdoc: the latter appointment is not established by the source. ([Google Sites][10])

**Diankun Liu** is another useful person to ask Yakun about. A paper published online in February 2026, *Curved Kakeya Sets and Nikodym Problems on Manifolds*, lists Liu and Yakun Xi as Zhejiang University authors. The scientific fit is excellent, but I could verify only the affiliation and research connection—not whether Liu is currently a PhD student or postdoc. He should therefore remain a **lead requiring confirmation**, rather than being assigned a career stage in the proposal. ([HEP Journal][11])

For the remaining junior names, I would ask Yakun and Meng to nominate current students whose projects match the programme. That is more reliable than inferring supervisory relationships or student status from coauthorship.

## 4. Suggested proposal-ready version

This version adds Cao and Li without turning the section into a directory. The final paragraph proposes specific engagement activities; retain those that you intend to arrange.

```latex
\section{Engagement with research strengths near Hangzhou}

The proposed workshop would engage directly with the strengths in
harmonic analysis and related areas at Zhejiang University and nearby
institutions in Hangzhou. At Zhejiang University, Yakun Xi and Meng Wang
provide natural connections to the local harmonic-analysis community.
Xi's work on Kakeya--Nikodym problems and eigenfunction estimates offers
particularly close links to the workshop's scientific scope. Further
opportunities for interaction are provided by Zhongwei Shen at Westlake
University, whose research connects harmonic analysis with partial
differential equations, and Jun Cao at Zhejiang University of Technology,
whose work includes function spaces, elliptic operators, and heat-kernel
estimates. These complementary strengths create a natural setting for
exchanges between harmonic analysis, spectral theory, and PDEs.

The high-speed rail connection between Hangzhou and Shanghai would also
facilitate participation from Fudan University, including Xi Chen at the
Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences, together with Danqing He,
Hongquan Li, and Bobo Hua. Their research provides connections to
microlocal and spectral analysis, multilinear harmonic analysis,
analysis on manifolds, and geometric and discrete analysis. The workshop
would build on existing regional exchanges: for example, a
harmonic-analysis workshop at SCMS in September 2025 was co-organized by
Xi Chen and Danqing He and listed Yakun Xi and Zhongwei Shen among its
speakers.

To translate these scientific connections into local engagement, we
plan to circulate the workshop announcement through the relevant
research groups and invite nominations of interested PhD students and
postdoctoral researchers. Zhejiang University's early-career community
includes Xuerui Yang, whose research connects harmonic analysis with
analytic number theory. In coordination with IASM and subject to
capacity, we would seek to involve local early-career researchers in
selected talks, problem sessions, and informal discussions with invited
participants. This would provide opportunities to present ongoing work,
receive feedback, and develop collaborations extending beyond the
workshop.
```

The named research connections are supported by the profiles and publications above; the reference to the September 2025 workshop is supported by its official programme. ([Zhejiang University Personal Page][6])

An optional sentence, particularly useful for a broader harmonic-analysis programme, is:

```latex
Additional opportunities for local engagement include Xiangxing Tao
at Zhejiang University of Science and Technology, through his work in
real-variable harmonic analysis, and Yanqi Qiu at the Hangzhou Institute
for Advanced Study, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, through
his work on Fourier decay and random measures.
```

These research connections are documented in Tao’s faculty profile and Qiu’s recent work. ([ZUST][2])

Finally, I would keep **“high-speed rail connection”** rather than claim that Shanghai participants can reach IASM within an hour. IASM’s travel guidance gives approximately one hour for the **Shanghai Hongqiao–Hangzhou East train segment**, not the full journey between university campuses. I would also omit “in person or online” unless a hybrid format has been agreed, and list these individuals as confirmed participants only after obtaining their agreement. ([iasm.zju.edu.cn][12])

